WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:04.200
In the last month or two, there's been a measurable
increase in the attention to a wide variety

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:06.160
of smaller math channels on YouTube.

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:10.970
My friend James and I ran a second iteration
of a contest that we did last year, the Summer

00:00:10.970 --> 00:00:15.420
of Math Exposition which invites people to
put up lessons about math online.

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:19.290
Could be a video, could be an article - any
medium you dream up, whatever topic you dream

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:24.011
up and we have some prizes available for the
ones that we deem, in some sense best, whatever

00:00:24.011 --> 00:00:25.011
that could mean.

00:00:25.011 --> 00:00:29.260
The deadline for submissions was a little
over a month ago and if we just focus on the

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:34.880
video entries, they've collectively accumulated
over 7 million views since that time.

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:38.410
Considering that the vast majority of these
are uploaded to very young channels where

00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:42.600
the video is often just the first or the second
upload, this was really exciting for me to

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:43.600
see.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:47.829
I suspect a big part of the reason for this
rising tide is that after the submission deadline,

00:00:47.829 --> 00:00:52.079
we ran a peer review process where an algorithm
would feed participants two different videos

00:00:52.079 --> 00:00:56.590
to compare and they'd be asked to vote which
one of these is "better" according to a few

00:00:56.590 --> 00:00:57.590
criteria.

00:00:57.590 --> 00:01:03.190
Now, that process generated over 10,000 comparisons
which, on the one hand helps to provide an

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:07.610
initial rough rank ordering of all the videos
but more important than that, any judgments

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:12.820
or rankings, it gave an excuse for many hundreds
of people to upload around a similar time

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:16.610
and then collectively view each other's work
helping to jump start a cluster of videos

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:18.540
with a shared viewer base.

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:22.610
And that 7 million number doesn't account
for other videos on these channels, for instance

00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:27.850
the many submissions people made to last year's
contest which since this year's deadline collectively

00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:30.200
jumped up by about 2 million views.

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:33.690
One group who told me that last year's contest
was what inspired them to put up their first

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:38.420
video mentioned to me that a video they had
made in between the two contests managed to

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:44.900
suddenly jump from 1000 to 600,000 views during
the peer review process for this year's contest.

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:47.150
Despite not being among those videos reviewed.

00:01:47.150 --> 00:01:51.110
This is all to say, there can be a surprising
value in the seemingly simple presence of

00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:52.920
a shared goal and a shared deadline.

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:57.030
And again, these are just the video entries
where it's easy for us to run these analytics

00:01:57.030 --> 00:01:58.820
to quickly get a sense of the reach.

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:01.580
Many of my favorite entries were the written
ones.

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:05.590
The spirit of the contest, as you can no doubt
tell, is getting more people to put out math

00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:07.970
lessons and on that front, mission accomplished.

00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:12.980
But I did promise to select five winners - lessons
that stood out as especially valuable for

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:16.819
one reason or another and that brings us to
this video here.

00:02:16.819 --> 00:02:20.189
To choose winners, James and I both spent
a couple weeks giving a pretty thorough look

00:02:20.189 --> 00:02:25.499
at over 100 of the top entries as determined
by the peer review process and we also recruited

00:02:25.499 --> 00:02:29.370
a few guest judges from the community to help
look at a subset of these and make sure that

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:32.439
our own biases and blind spots aren't playing
too heavy a role.

00:02:32.439 --> 00:02:34.419
Many, many things to them in the time they
offered.

00:02:34.419 --> 00:02:37.909
I won't tell you the final decision until
the end of the video.

00:02:37.909 --> 00:02:41.569
What I thought might be more fun is to lead
up to it by talking through the criteria that

00:02:41.569 --> 00:02:46.809
I had in mind when making this election, highlighting
as many exemplary submissions as I can along

00:02:46.809 --> 00:02:47.809
the way.

00:02:47.809 --> 00:02:51.530
Hopefully giving any of you who are looking
to put out your own math lessons online at

00:02:51.530 --> 00:02:53.989
some point a few concrete things to focus
on.

00:02:53.989 --> 00:02:59.290
At a high level, the four criteria I told
people I'd look out for were motivation, clarity,

00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:01.370
novelty and memorability.

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:05.079
The first two are probably the most important
and let's start with motivation.

00:03:05.079 --> 00:03:08.779
This actually has two meanings I can think
of - one on the macro scale and one on the

00:03:08.779 --> 00:03:10.099
micro scale.

00:03:10.099 --> 00:03:15.790
By macro scale motivation, I mean how well
do you hook someone into the lesson as a whole.

00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:20.329
This video by Aleksandr Berdnikov opens by
asking why it is that when you hear a plane

00:03:20.329 --> 00:03:24.700
approaching, the pitch of its sound seems
to slowly fall.

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:28.650
He points out how a lot of people assumed
this is the Doppler Effect, but that this

00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:32.749
doesn't actually hold up to scrutiny since
for example, that pitch actually rises as

00:03:32.749 --> 00:03:34.150
the plane is going away from you.

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:38.829
It's a good point and an interesting question,
you have my attention.

00:03:38.829 --> 00:03:42.529
One of my favorite articles in the batch by
Adi Mittal prompts you to wonder about an

00:03:42.529 --> 00:03:47.790
algorithm behind the panorama feature on your
phone and proceeds to explain one - why that's

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:52.859
not trivial and then two, the linear algebra
and projective geometry involved in a DIY

00:03:52.859 --> 00:03:57.709
style project to stitch together two overlapping
images taken a different angles.

00:03:57.709 --> 00:04:01.379
Application and tangible problems can make
for great motivation, but that's not the only

00:04:01.379 --> 00:04:02.939
source of motivation.

00:04:02.939 --> 00:04:07.449
Depending on the target audience, a good nerd
sniping question can also do the trick.

00:04:07.449 --> 00:04:12.269
This video on the channel Going Null opens
with a seemingly impossible puzzle.

00:04:12.269 --> 00:04:17.590
Ten prisoners are each given a hat chosen
arbitrarily from ten total hat types available.

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:21.510
It is possible for some of the prisoners to
have the same hat type as others and everyone

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:24.480
can see all of the other hats but not their
own.

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:28.540
After given a little time to look everyone
else's hat and think about it all, the prisoners

00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:32.530
are to simultaneously shout out a guest for
their own hat type.

00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:36.820
The question is, can you find a method that
guarantees at least one of the prisoners will

00:04:36.820 --> 00:04:40.530
make a correct guess?

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:45.600
This video by Eric Rowland motivates the idea
of p-adic numbers than the p-adic metric by

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:51.880
showing how if you take 2^10th and 2^100th,
2^1000th, so on and so on and you assign distinct

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:55.350
colors to each digit lining them all up on
the right.

00:04:55.350 --> 00:05:00.000
You can see that their final digits line up
more and more with larger powers.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.000
Then he asks the question of whether it's
reasonable to interpret this as a kind of

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:08.790
convergence, despite the fact that these numbers
are clearly diverging to infinity in the usual

00:05:08.790 --> 00:05:10.540
sense.

00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:14.760
A completely different form of motivation
can come from showing the historical significance

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:18.720
of a problem or field in a sense giving the
viewer a feeling that they are part of something

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:19.880
bigger.

00:05:19.880 --> 00:05:24.320
One excellent video on a channel A Well-Rested
Dog provides an overview of the history of

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:28.280
calculus and the progression of how some of
the world's smartest minds grappled with the

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:31.000
nuances of infinity and infinitesimals.

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:34.500
It's the right mixture of entertaining and
detailed and he goes on to talk about how

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:39.050
learning all of this made his own questions
and confusions in a calculus class feel validated.

00:05:39.050 --> 00:05:42.020
Which I think a of students can resonate with.

00:05:42.020 --> 00:05:46.180
This example is less about the intro of a
video motivating the lesson it teaches and

00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:49.690
more about the entire video motivating an
entire field.

00:05:49.690 --> 00:05:54.590
And another one like that would be this lecture
by the channel Thricery, laying out how Cantor's

00:05:54.590 --> 00:05:59.330
diagonalization argument and the Halting Problem
and a number of other paradoxes people might

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:02.140
have heard of in math, computer science and
logic.

00:06:02.140 --> 00:06:06.440
All actually follow the same basic pattern
and moreover, if you try to formalize the

00:06:06.440 --> 00:06:10.490
exact sense in which they follow the same
pattern, that ends up serving is a pretty

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:13.460
nice motivation for the subject of Category
Theory.

00:06:13.460 --> 00:06:18.680
And the last flavour of motivation I'll mention
is if you can somehow make the learner feel

00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:21.470
like they're playing an active role in the
lesson.

00:06:21.470 --> 00:06:25.890
This is very hard to do with a video, may
be even impossible, and it's best suited for

00:06:25.890 --> 00:06:30.490
in-person lessons but one written entry that
I thought did this especially well was an

00:06:30.490 --> 00:06:34.770
inverse turing test, where you as the reader
are challenged to come up with a sequence

00:06:34.770 --> 00:06:40.080
of ones and zeros that appears random and
the article goes on to explain various statistical

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:44.450
tests that you could apply to prove that the
sequence was actually human generated and

00:06:44.450 --> 00:06:46.200
not really random.

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:50.450
The content of the article is centered around
the particular sequence that you, the reader

00:06:50.450 --> 00:06:54.920
created and you're invited to change it along
the way to try to get it to pass more tests.

00:06:54.920 --> 00:07:00.080
It's a nice touch and I could easily see this
working really well as a classroom activity.

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:03.420
Whatever approach you take, whatever flavor
of motivation is your favorite, it's hard

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:08.560
to overstate just how important it is that
you do actually give viewers a reason to care.

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:12.520
This is true for any piece of content but
I think it's especially true for educational

00:07:12.520 --> 00:07:17.420
content and even more so for math given the
amount of focus and thought that these topics

00:07:17.420 --> 00:07:19.150
sometimes require.

00:07:19.150 --> 00:07:23.270
I think this was articulated best by the author
of one of my favorite podcasts, An Opinionated

00:07:23.270 --> 00:07:27.800
History of Mathematics, who has a manifesto
on his website that lays out what he calls

00:07:27.800 --> 00:07:33.180
"the axioms of learning" beginning with the
first axiom, "In a perfect world, students

00:07:33.180 --> 00:07:37.360
pursue learning not because it is prescribed
to them but rather out of a genuine desire

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:38.880
to figure things out.

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:43.260
It follows that we must not introduce any
topic for which we cannot first convince the

00:07:43.260 --> 00:07:46.000
students that they should want to pursue it."

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:51.919
That said, one mistake that I think I've made
in past videos is to over philosophize in

00:07:51.919 --> 00:07:54.110
the videos introduction.

00:07:54.110 --> 00:07:57.800
Motivation is critical but it doesn't have
to take long, and often what actually keeps

00:07:57.800 --> 00:08:01.590
the viewer engaged is to get right to the
point and leave any commentary about broader

00:08:01.590 --> 00:08:03.920
themes and connections to the end.

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:08.640
If you can, motivate using clear examples,
not sweeping statements or promises of what

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:10.560
is to come.

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:14.690
By micro scale motivation, what I mean is
whether each new idea that's introduced in

00:08:14.690 --> 00:08:18.890
the lesson itself feels to the learner like
it has a good reason to be there.

00:08:18.890 --> 00:08:23.710
For instance, this video by Joshua Maros gives
a fairly detailed overview of Ray Tracing

00:08:23.710 --> 00:08:28.070
and what I love about it is that before he
introduces any new technical topic, like the

00:08:28.070 --> 00:08:33.039
rendering equation, important sampling or
the ReSTIR algorithm, he's already outlined

00:08:33.039 --> 00:08:38.070
the main idea and intuition for that topic
with really well visualized examples.

00:08:38.070 --> 00:08:42.909
It makes it so that once the equation on the
screen or the algorithm is described, it doesn't

00:08:42.909 --> 00:08:45.910
feel like an expression handed down with nothing
to hold onto.

00:08:45.910 --> 00:08:50.819
Instead, it arrives only once it's articulating
something that already exists at least loosely

00:08:50.819 --> 00:08:55.100
in the viewer’s mind, making it much easier
to parse.

00:08:55.100 --> 00:08:59.709
This video by Michael DiFranco about extending
the factorial offers another great example

00:08:59.709 --> 00:09:02.360
of a lesson with good motivation along the
way.

00:09:02.360 --> 00:09:06.019
You may have heard that there is a function
generalizing the factorial to real and even

00:09:06.019 --> 00:09:08.329
complex inputs, the gamma function.

00:09:08.329 --> 00:09:12.490
The usual definition is written down as a
certain integral expression sort of handed

00:09:12.490 --> 00:09:17.640
down from on high and the justification for
why this generalizes factorials is certain

00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:21.959
properties that you can prove about it but
a lot of students find this unsatisfying.

00:09:21.959 --> 00:09:22.999
Where does it come from?

00:09:22.999 --> 00:09:27.759
By contrast in Michael's explanation, he starts
by observing the properties that are true

00:09:27.759 --> 00:09:32.680
of the normal factorial function that you
would want to be true of a general version

00:09:32.680 --> 00:09:37.399
and uses those desired properties to motivate
various different alternate expressions massaged

00:09:37.399 --> 00:09:41.779
here and there to be more amenable to whole
number inputs ultimately leading to a pretty

00:09:41.779 --> 00:09:44.360
satisfying answer.

00:09:44.360 --> 00:09:48.220
Another good template for this micro scale
motivation when introducing a pretty complicated

00:09:48.220 --> 00:09:53.339
solution to a problem is to start with a naive
but flawed solution and then progressively

00:09:53.339 --> 00:09:54.709
refine it.

00:09:54.709 --> 00:09:59.709
This article by Max Slater on differential
programming does this particularly well.

00:09:59.709 --> 00:10:04.360
The basic question is how you get computers
to evaluate derivatives - a ubiquitous task

00:10:04.360 --> 00:10:05.600
for machine learning.

00:10:05.600 --> 00:10:11.420
He starts by describing the most obvious approach
and then what flaws it has and uses that as

00:10:11.420 --> 00:10:13.130
for another approach.

00:10:13.130 --> 00:10:16.869
But that one has its own flaws and fixing
those motivates yet another approach and so

00:10:16.869 --> 00:10:17.869
on.

00:10:17.869 --> 00:10:23.040
The ideas he builds up to, dual numbers and
backward mode automatic differentiation, both

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:26.069
could feel a bit confusing if presented out
of the blue.

00:10:26.069 --> 00:10:30.709
But in context, having motivated each new
idea by pointing out flaws with the previous

00:10:30.709 --> 00:10:33.910
ones, it all ends up feeling utterly reasonable.

00:10:33.910 --> 00:10:37.649
Turning back to that same prisoner hat puzzle
I referenced earlier, one of the other things

00:10:37.649 --> 00:10:40.619
I liked about it is how the author doesn't
just present the solution.

00:10:40.619 --> 00:10:43.689
There are plenty of puzzle videos out there
which do that.

00:10:43.689 --> 00:10:47.750
Instead, he gives a pretty authentic look
at the wrong turns and tangents that are involved

00:10:47.750 --> 00:10:52.369
in the problem solving process, not even eating
up too much time to do so, and he justifies

00:10:52.369 --> 00:10:55.830
each new step with a general problem solving
principle.

00:10:55.830 --> 00:11:01.180
All of this micro scale motivation could just
as well be categorized as a subset of clarity.

00:11:01.180 --> 00:11:04.920
If motivating a lesson determines how much
attention and focus the viewer is willing

00:11:04.920 --> 00:11:09.380
to give you, clarity determines how quickly
you burn through that focus.

00:11:09.380 --> 00:11:14.199
The best hook in the world is wasted if the
lesson which follows is confusing.

00:11:14.199 --> 00:11:18.970
This presentation by Ex Planaria talks about
how to describe various crystal structures

00:11:18.970 --> 00:11:24.220
using group theory, which considering the
complex 3D forms involved and the fact that

00:11:24.220 --> 00:11:28.540
most people don't know Group Theory, has the
potential to be very confusing.

00:11:28.540 --> 00:11:32.819
But they do a really effective job at keeping
concrete examples front and centre guiding

00:11:32.819 --> 00:11:37.050
the reader to focus on one relevant pattern
at a time and distilling down to a simple

00:11:37.050 --> 00:11:41.980
version of an idea before seeing how that
fits into a broader more general setting.

00:11:41.980 --> 00:11:46.709
In general, entries that struck me is especially
clear would often keep one or two examples

00:11:46.709 --> 00:11:51.249
front and center and that often give a feeling
of playing with those examples - may be running

00:11:51.249 --> 00:11:55.610
simulations or tweaking them to run up against
edge cases and all around giving the viewer

00:11:55.610 --> 00:12:00.319
a chance to build their own intuitions before
general rules are presented.

00:12:00.319 --> 00:12:03.079
The example doesn't even have to be explicit.

00:12:03.079 --> 00:12:07.550
In a visually driven lesson, the choice of
what to show on screen when making general

00:12:07.550 --> 00:12:12.649
points is often a great opportunity to offer
the viewer a concrete example to hold on but

00:12:12.649 --> 00:12:17.509
without wasting too much time explicitly talking
about that example or over emphasizing its

00:12:17.509 --> 00:12:18.509
importance.

00:12:18.509 --> 00:12:23.119
This I think is part of what gives visually
driven lessons the opportunity to be clearer.

00:12:23.119 --> 00:12:26.809
As a brief side comment by the way loosely
related to clarity - for any of you who want

00:12:26.809 --> 00:12:32.009
to use music in the videos, while music can
enrich the story telling aspect of a lesson

00:12:32.009 --> 00:12:37.110
- setting the desired tone and the momentum,
once you're getting into the meat of a technical

00:12:37.110 --> 00:12:40.829
explanation, it's very easy for the music
to do more harm than good.

00:12:40.829 --> 00:12:44.809
If it's there at all, you want it to be decidedly
in the background and not calling attention

00:12:44.809 --> 00:12:45.809
to itself.

00:12:45.809 --> 00:12:49.569
I recognize some hypocrisy here, it's definitely
something I know I've messed up with past

00:12:49.569 --> 00:12:53.509
videos and it's just worth thinking about
whatever benefit you see from the music.

00:12:53.509 --> 00:12:57.509
You don't want to incur a needless cost on
clarity that outweighs that benefit.

00:12:57.509 --> 00:13:03.149
Moving on to novelty, this is another category
that has two distinct interpretations.

00:13:03.149 --> 00:13:05.810
One would be stylistic originality.

00:13:05.810 --> 00:13:09.709
Back when I created this channel, part of
the reason I wrote my own animation tool behind

00:13:09.709 --> 00:13:12.930
it was to ensure a kind of stylistic originality.

00:13:12.930 --> 00:13:17.509
Well, the main reason was it was a fun side
project and having my hands deep into the

00:13:17.509 --> 00:13:22.770
guts of some tool helped me to feel less constrained
in trying to visualize whatever came to mind.

00:13:22.770 --> 00:13:27.629
But being a forcing function for originality
was at least a small part of my reasoning.

00:13:27.629 --> 00:13:31.579
This means there's at least a little hint
of irony in the fact that if we fast forward

00:13:31.579 --> 00:13:36.779
to today, so many of the entries in this contest
used that tool (Manim) to illustrate their

00:13:36.779 --> 00:13:37.779
lessons.

00:13:37.779 --> 00:13:38.779
I have nothing wrong with that.

00:13:38.779 --> 00:13:39.779
It actually delights me.

00:13:39.779 --> 00:13:43.559
It's why I made it open so and I'm very grateful
to the Manim community for everything they've

00:13:43.559 --> 00:13:46.050
done to make the tool more accessible.

00:13:46.050 --> 00:13:49.879
But I would still encourage people to find
their own unique voice and aesthetic whatever

00:13:49.879 --> 00:13:53.050
tools they use and whoever they take inspiration
from.

00:13:53.050 --> 00:13:56.559
I don't want to over emphasize that point
because it's the much less important half

00:13:56.559 --> 00:13:57.739
of novelty.

00:13:57.739 --> 00:14:01.499
The much more important kind of novelty is
when the thing you present would have been

00:14:01.499 --> 00:14:03.860
very hard to find elsewhere on the internet.

00:14:03.860 --> 00:14:08.800
Either because it's a highly unique topic
or because it's a very unique perspective.

00:14:08.800 --> 00:14:14.139
For example, this video on percolation showed
a completely fascinating toy model for studying

00:14:14.139 --> 00:14:18.209
phase changes, a model where it's easier to
make exact proofs.

00:14:18.209 --> 00:14:21.959
And considering the level of depth and the
level of clarity that the authors provided,

00:14:21.959 --> 00:14:25.119
I think it's fair to say you wouldn't find
something like this on YouTube if this group

00:14:25.119 --> 00:14:26.569
hadn't made it.

00:14:26.569 --> 00:14:29.750
As to memorability, I'll keep this one quick.

00:14:29.750 --> 00:14:33.749
Lessons take off this box when they ask a
question that's just so fun to think about

00:14:33.749 --> 00:14:38.259
or provide such a satisfying aha moment that
it stays with you long after watching it or

00:14:38.259 --> 00:14:39.259
reading it.

00:14:39.259 --> 00:14:41.699
Admittedly, this one is highly personal and
subjective.

00:14:41.699 --> 00:14:47.019
To my taste for example, this video by Daria
Ivanova discusses the question of when it's

00:14:47.019 --> 00:14:51.699
possible for a single track to have been left
by a bicycle - that is, the back wheel goes

00:14:51.699 --> 00:14:57.309
through the same path that the front wheel
does, which is just so fun to think about.

00:14:57.309 --> 00:15:01.910
This video by Gergely Bencsik about how involute
gears work had a really satisfying way of

00:15:01.910 --> 00:15:06.860
explaining why a certain gear design pattern
works so well that for me at least just stuck.

00:15:06.860 --> 00:15:10.360
So, with all of that, who are chosen winners?

00:15:10.360 --> 00:15:13.889
In the announcement, I promised that one winner
slot would go to an entry that was made as

00:15:13.889 --> 00:15:17.679
a collaboration and that one goes to the percolation
video.

00:15:17.679 --> 00:15:21.850
The other four winners are, perhaps unsurprisingly,
also ones that I've already mentioned.

00:15:21.850 --> 00:15:26.410
They include the post about describing crystal
structures with group theory, the video covering

00:15:26.410 --> 00:15:30.170
the history of calculus, the one about ray
tracing and the algorithms to make it faster

00:15:30.170 --> 00:15:34.410
and the problem solving lesson centered around
the tricky hat riddle.

00:15:34.410 --> 00:15:36.209
These entries really do speak for themselves.

00:15:36.209 --> 00:15:40.120
So, rather than telling you too much more
here, I encourage to check them out.

00:15:40.120 --> 00:15:44.589
To be honest, after I got it down to about
25 entries that I wanted to at least be honorable

00:15:44.589 --> 00:15:48.449
mentions, it was exceedingly hard to actually
choose winners from that.

00:15:48.449 --> 00:15:52.160
Since for each of these, I could easily envision
a target audience for whom that entry would

00:15:52.160 --> 00:15:54.000
actually be the best recommendation.

00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:58.059
It was a game of comparing apples to oranges
but times twenty-five.

00:15:58.059 --> 00:16:02.309
Below the video, I've left links to the other
20 that I chose as honorable mentions and

00:16:02.309 --> 00:16:07.040
to a playlist that contains all the video
submissions and also to a blog post containing

00:16:07.040 --> 00:16:10.059
links to all of the non-video submissions.

00:16:10.059 --> 00:16:11.610
Thanks to a sponsorship from Brilliant.

00:16:11.610 --> 00:16:15.480
Each winner will get a thousand dollars as
a cash prize and also - and much more importantly

00:16:15.480 --> 00:16:17.949
I think - a rare addition golden pie creature.

00:16:17.949 --> 00:16:22.749
Also, after the initial announcement, Risk
Zero and Google Fonts both generously reached

00:16:22.749 --> 00:16:24.879
out offering additional prize sponsorships.

00:16:24.879 --> 00:16:29.199
I'd also like to thank Protocol Labs for another
contribution to help us cover the cost of

00:16:29.199 --> 00:16:30.589
managing the whole event.

00:16:30.589 --> 00:16:35.190
Thanks to everybody who participated and to
everybody who helped in creating this rising

00:16:35.190 --> 00:16:39.320
tide for new math channels and new math blogs
that we've seen in the last month.

00:16:39.320 --> 00:16:42.350
It was genuinely inspiring to see just how
well this all went.

